(function (T) { T.unit({ id: "7.4", title: "Binary and data", q: "How can a machine that only knows on and off store numbers?", lessons: [
{ t: "Why computers use two states", o: "I can explain why computers store everything in binary.",
  k: ["binary: a number system that uses only the digits 0 and 1", "bit: one binary digit, a 0 or a 1", "transistor: a tiny electronic switch that is either on or off"],
  d: ["A light switch has two positions. How many different messages could you send with one switch?", "Two: on or off."],
  s: ["Switches|A processor contains billions of transistors|Each transistor is a switch: on or off|On is written as 1, off as 0",
      "Why only two states?|Two states are easy to tell apart, even with electrical noise|Ten different voltage levels would be easily confused|Simple, cheap and reliable",
      "Everything is binary|Numbers, text, pictures, sound and video|All stored as patterns of 0s and 1s|The program decides what a pattern means"],
  a: "Using a torch or desk lamp as a switch, send your partner a secret yes or no answer, then work out how to send one of four answers using two flashes (on or off each). Record your code table. Support: complete the two-switch table on the sheet. Stretch: how many different messages can three switches send? Four? Find the rule.",
  w: [["What is a bit?", "One binary digit: a 0 or a 1."], ["Which two digits does binary use?", "0 and 1."], ["What does a transistor do in a computer?", "It acts as a tiny switch that is on or off."], ["How many different patterns can two switches make? List them.", "Four: 00, 01, 10, 11."], ["Explain why a computer uses two voltage levels and not ten.", "Two levels are easy to tell apart reliably. Ten close levels could be misread when there is electrical noise."]],
  x: ["Complete: on is written as __ and off is written as __.", "1 and 0."] },
{ t: "Counting in binary", o: "I can count in binary using place values.",
  k: ["denary: the base 10 number system we use every day", "place value: what a digit is worth because of its position", "base 2: another name for binary, where each place is worth double the one to its right"],
  d: ["In the denary number 352, what is the 5 worth?", "50, because it is in the tens place."],
  s: ["Place values|Denary places: 100, 10, 1 (each ten times bigger)|Binary places: 8, 4, 2, 1 (each two times bigger)|For 8 bits: 128, 64, 32, 16, 8, 4, 2, 1",
      "Counting|0000, 0001, 0010, 0011, 0100|When a column is full (1), reset it to 0 and carry 1 to the left|The right-hand bit flips every time",
      "Using cards|Hold up the cards 8, 4, 2, 1|A card showing its dots is a 1; face down is a 0|Add up the dots you can see"],
  a: "With the place-value cards, count from 0 to 15 as a table group, writing each number in binary. Then write the numbers 16 to 20 using five bits. Support: use the card mat with the values printed. Stretch: what is the largest number you can show with five cards? Six?",
  w: [["Write the place values for a 4-bit binary number, largest first.", "8, 4, 2, 1."], ["What is denary?", "The base 10 number system used every day."], ["Write the binary for 5 using 4 bits.", "0101."], ["What number comes after 0111 in binary?", "1000."], ["Explain why the place values in binary double each time.", "Each place can hold only 0 or 1, so the next place must be worth two of the place before."]],
  x: ["Count from 0 to 7 in binary using three bits.", "000, 001, 010, 011, 100, 101, 110, 111."] },
{ t: "Lab: human binary counter and binary bracelets", o: "I can represent numbers in binary in more than one way.",
  k: ["represent: to stand for or show something", "pattern: an arrangement that follows a rule", "code: a system where symbols stand for something else"],
  d: ["Five people stand in a row. Standing means 1 and sitting means 0. The row is: stand, sit, stand, sit, sit. What number is it?", "10100 = 16 + 4 = 20."],
  s: ["Human counter|Each person is one bit, holding a place-value card|Standing is 1, sitting is 0|Count up together: who has to move most often?",
      "What you notice|The person on the right moves every count|The next moves every 2 counts, then every 4, then every 8|This is the doubling pattern",
      "Binary bracelets|Choose two bead colours: one for 1, one for 0|Each letter of your initials is a number: A = 1, B = 2, up to Z = 26|Thread 5 bits per letter"],
  a: "Take part in the human binary counter up to 31. Then encode your initials as 5-bit numbers (A = 1 to Z = 26) and make or draw your bracelet. Swap and decode a partner's. Support: use the letter-to-number chart with binary filled in for A to H. Stretch: encode a five-letter word for your partner to decode.",
  w: [["In the human counter, how often does the right-hand person move?", "Every count."], ["How many bits are needed to give each of 26 letters a different number?", "5, because 5 bits give 32 patterns."], ["If A = 1, what letter is 00011?", "C."], ["Write the letter E (5) as a 5-bit binary number.", "00101."], ["Explain why 4 bits are not enough for the alphabet.", "4 bits give only 16 patterns, fewer than 26 letters."]],
  x: ["Write your first initial as a 5-bit binary number.", "The correct 5-bit code for the student's letter."] },
{ t: "Binary to denary", o: "I can convert an 8-bit binary number into denary.",
  k: ["convert: to change from one form to another", "byte: a group of 8 bits", "most significant bit: the left-hand bit, with the largest place value"],
  d: ["Write the eight place values of a byte, largest first.", "128, 64, 32, 16, 8, 4, 2, 1."],
  s: ["The method|Write the place values above the bits|Circle the place values that have a 1 under them|Add the circled values",
      "Worked example: 01001101|Place values with a 1: 64, 8, 4, 1|64 + 8 + 4 + 1 = 77|Check: the last bit is 1, so the answer must be odd",
      "Quick checks|All zeros is 0; all ones is 255|If the left-hand bit is 1, the number is 128 or more|If the right-hand bit is 1, the number is odd"],
  a: "Convert the 15 binary numbers on the sheet to denary, showing the place values you added. Mark your own work using the checks. Support: use the printed place-value grid for every question. Stretch: without converting fully, sort six bytes from smallest to largest and explain your method.",
  w: [["Convert 00001101 to denary.", "13 (8 + 4 + 1)."], ["Convert 01001101 to denary.", "77 (64 + 8 + 4 + 1)."], ["Convert 10010110 to denary.", "150 (128 + 16 + 4 + 2)."], ["What is the largest denary number one byte can hold? Show why.", "255, because 128 + 64 + 32 + 16 + 8 + 4 + 2 + 1 = 255."], ["Without converting, explain how you know 10000000 is bigger than 01111111.", "The first has the 128 bit set. The second adds up to only 127, all the smaller places together."]],
  x: ["Convert 00101010 to denary.", "42."] },
{ t: "Denary to binary", o: "I can convert a denary number up to 255 into 8-bit binary.",
  k: ["subtract: to take away", "remainder: what is left after taking away", "leading zero: a 0 at the left that fills the byte to 8 bits"],
  d: ["Which place values add up to 20?", "16 and 4."],
  s: ["Subtract the largest|Start at 128 and work to the right|If the place value fits into what is left, write 1 and subtract it|If it does not fit, write 0",
      "Worked example: 200|128 fits: 1, leaves 72. 64 fits: 1, leaves 8|32 no: 0. 16 no: 0. 8 fits: 1, leaves 0|4, 2, 1 no: 0, 0, 0. Answer 11001000",
      "Check your answer|Convert it back to denary|Always write all 8 bits, with leading zeros|An even number must end in 0"],
  a: "Convert the 15 denary numbers to 8-bit binary using the subtract-the-largest method, then check five of them by converting back. Support: use the conversion frame that has a box for each place value. Stretch: convert 256 and 300. What goes wrong with 8 bits, and how many bits are needed?",
  w: [["Convert 45 to 8-bit binary.", "00101101."], ["Convert 99 to 8-bit binary.", "01100011."], ["Convert 200 to 8-bit binary.", "11001000."], ["A student writes 12 as 1100. What is missing if the answer must be a byte?", "Four leading zeros: 00001100."], ["Explain why 256 cannot be stored in one byte.", "The largest 8-bit value is 255. 256 needs a ninth bit worth 256."]],
  x: ["Convert 18 to 8-bit binary.", "00010010."] },
{ t: "Lab: conversion game and leaderboard", o: "I can convert between binary and denary quickly and accurately.",
  k: ["fluent: able to do something quickly and correctly", "accuracy: how many answers are correct", "strategy: a planned way of doing something"],
  d: ["Convert 11111111 and 00000001 to denary.", "255 and 1."],
  s: ["Speed strategies|Learn the place values by heart|Spot patterns: 1111 is 15, 10000000 is 128|For numbers near 255, subtract the missing places from 255",
      "The game|Round 1: binary to denary, 10 questions|Round 2: denary to binary, 10 questions|Score = correct answers; time decides ties",
      "After each round|Mark with your partner|Circle any mistake and find where it happened|Try to beat your own score, not just others"],
  a: "Play three rounds of the conversion game using the Data page of CS Toolkit to check answers after each round. Record your scores and times on the class leaderboard. Support: play with 4-bit numbers first. Stretch: play the round with a 10-second limit per question.",
  w: [["Convert 11110000 to denary.", "240."], ["Convert 129 to binary.", "10000001."], ["Convert 01111111 to denary.", "127."], ["Convert 64 to binary.", "01000000."], ["Explain a quick way to convert 11111110 without adding seven numbers.", "It is 255 with the 1 bit missing, so 255 - 1 = 254."]],
  x: ["What was your best score, and which type of question do you still find harder?", "A score and a type."] },
{ t: "Binary addition rules", o: "I can add two binary numbers using the four rules.",
  k: ["sum: the result of an addition", "carry: a 1 moved into the next column to the left", "column: one place-value position"],
  d: ["Work out 7 + 5 in denary. What did you do when the column total was more than 9?", "12: write 2 and carry 1 into the tens."],
  s: ["The four rules|0 + 0 = 0|0 + 1 = 1|1 + 1 = 0 carry 1|1 + 1 + 1 = 1 carry 1",
      "Method|Line the numbers up on the right|Add from the right-hand column|Write carries small, above the next column",
      "Worked example|0101 + 0011|Right to left: 1+1 = 0 carry 1; 0+1+1 = 0 carry 1; 1+0+1 = 0 carry 1; 0+0+1 = 1|Answer 1000. Check: 5 + 3 = 8"],
  a: "Complete the 12 four-bit additions, showing every carry, and check each in denary. Support: use the grid with a carry row. Stretch: add three binary numbers at once and explain what happens when a column totals 4.",
  w: [["What is 1 + 1 in binary?", "10: write 0, carry 1."], ["What is 1 + 1 + 1 in binary?", "11: write 1, carry 1."], ["Add 0101 + 0011.", "1000 (5 + 3 = 8)."], ["Add 0110 + 0111 and check in denary.", "1101 (6 + 7 = 13)."], ["Explain why 1 + 1 gives a carry in binary.", "The answer 2 is too big for one column, which can hold only 0 or 1, so it becomes one in the next column."]],
  x: ["Add 0011 + 0011.", "0110 (3 + 3 = 6)."] },
{ t: "Carrying and 8-bit overflow", o: "I can add two bytes and recognise an overflow error.",
  k: ["overflow: when the result of a calculation is too big to store", "register: a small store inside the processor that holds a fixed number of bits", "error: a wrong result"],
  d: ["What is the largest number one byte can hold?", "255."],
  s: ["Adding bytes|The same four rules, across eight columns|Example: 00110110 + 00011011 = 01010001|Check: 54 + 27 = 81",
      "Overflow|10010110 + 01110101 gives a carry out of the left-hand column|The true answer is 267, which needs 9 bits|An 8-bit register keeps only 00001011, which is 11",
      "Why it matters|The computer stores the wrong answer without warning|Programs must use enough bits for the largest possible value|Famous bugs have been caused by overflow"],
  a: "Complete the eight byte additions. For each, state whether overflow occurs and give the value an 8-bit register would store. Check with the Data page of CS Toolkit. Support: additions 1 to 4 have no overflow. Stretch: find two bytes that add to exactly 255, and two that give the smallest possible overflow.",
  w: [["What is overflow?", "When the result of a calculation is too big to be stored in the bits available."], ["Add 00110110 + 00011011.", "01010001 (54 + 27 = 81)."], ["Add 10010110 + 01110101. What does an 8-bit register store?", "00001011 (11). The true answer 267 needs a ninth bit."], ["How can you tell from the working that overflow has happened?", "There is a carry out of the left-hand (128) column."], ["A game stores a score in one byte. The score is 250 and the player gains 10. Explain what happens.", "260 does not fit in 8 bits. The stored value wraps round to 4, so the score appears to drop."]],
  x: ["Does 11000000 + 01000000 overflow? Explain.", "Yes. 192 + 64 = 256, one more than a byte can hold."] },
{ t: "Lab: addition relay", o: "I can add binary numbers accurately under time pressure and check my answers.",
  k: ["relay: a team activity where each person does one part", "verify: to check that an answer is correct", "working: the steps shown to reach an answer"],
  d: ["Add 0111 + 0001.", "1000."],
  s: ["The relay|Teams of four, one sum at a time|Each person adds one pair of bytes and passes it on|The next person checks it in denary before starting theirs",
      "Checking|Convert both numbers and the answer to denary|If they do not match, send it back|Spot overflow and say so",
      "Common slips|Forgetting a carry|Misaligning the columns|Writing 1 + 1 = 2"],
  a: "Complete the addition relay: 12 sums per team, with every answer verified in denary by the next team member. Then individually complete the five-question accuracy check. Support: your team uses the 4-bit relay cards. Stretch: write three sums that would catch out someone who forgets carries.",
  w: [["Add 00001111 + 00000001.", "00010000 (15 + 1 = 16)."], ["Add 01010101 + 00101010.", "01111111 (85 + 42 = 127)."], ["Add 01100100 + 00110010.", "10010110 (100 + 50 = 150)."], ["Add 11111111 + 00000001 in 8 bits. What is stored?", "00000000, with overflow. The true answer is 256."], ["Explain how checking in denary catches a missed carry.", "The denary total will not match the converted binary answer."]],
  x: ["Which slip did your team make most often?", "A named slip."] },
{ t: "Bits, nibbles, bytes and larger units", o: "I can use the units of data in the correct order.",
  k: ["nibble: 4 bits", "kibibyte (KiB): 1024 bytes", "mebibyte (MiB): 1024 kibibytes"],
  d: ["How many bits are in a byte?", "8."],
  s: ["Small units|Bit: one 0 or 1|Nibble: 4 bits|Byte: 8 bits, enough for one character",
      "Larger units|1 kibibyte (KiB) = 1024 bytes|1 mebibyte (MiB) = 1024 KiB|1 gibibyte (GiB) = 1024 MiB",
      "Why 1024?|Computers count in powers of 2|1024 is 2 multiplied by itself 10 times|It is the power of 2 closest to 1000"],
  a: "Order the unit cards from smallest to largest, then complete the conversion questions and match ten real files to a sensible size. Support: use the unit ladder diagram. Stretch: how many bytes are in 1 GiB? Write it as a calculation.",
  w: [["How many bits are in a nibble?", "4."], ["How many bytes are in a kibibyte?", "1024."], ["Put in order, smallest first: byte, mebibyte, bit, kibibyte, nibble.", "Bit, nibble, byte, kibibyte, mebibyte."], ["How many bits are in 3 bytes?", "24."], ["How many KiB are in 2 MiB? Show your working.", "2 x 1024 = 2048 KiB."]],
  x: ["Which unit would you use for the size of a photo: bytes, KiB or MiB?", "MiB (or KiB for a small one)."] },
{ t: "How many bits do I need?", o: "I can work out how many values a number of bits can represent.",
  k: ["range: the smallest to the largest value that can be stored", "power of 2: 2 multiplied by itself a number of times", "combination: one possible pattern of bits"],
  d: ["How many different patterns can 2 bits make? And 3 bits?", "4 and 8."],
  s: ["The rule|Each extra bit doubles the number of patterns|1 bit: 2. 2 bits: 4. 3 bits: 8. 4 bits: 16|n bits give 2 to the power n patterns",
      "The largest value|Counting starts at 0|So the largest number is one less than the number of patterns|8 bits: 256 patterns, values 0 to 255",
      "Choosing the number of bits|26 letters need 5 bits (32 patterns)|Marks from 0 to 100 need 7 bits (128 patterns)|Too few bits cause overflow; too many waste storage"],
  a: "Complete the table of bits, patterns and largest value for 1 to 10 bits. Then choose the number of bits needed for ten real situations (days of the week, months, a percentage, players in a class, and others). Support: complete the table to 8 bits. Stretch: how many bits would give every person on Earth (about 8 billion) a different number?",
  w: [["How many patterns can 4 bits make?", "16."], ["What is the largest number 5 bits can hold?", "31."], ["How many bits are needed to store a day of the week (7 values)?", "3 bits (8 patterns)."], ["How many bits are needed to store a mark from 0 to 100?", "7 bits (128 patterns)."], ["Explain why the largest 8-bit number is 255 and not 256.", "There are 256 patterns, but the first one stands for 0, so the last stands for 255."]],
  x: ["How many patterns do 6 bits give?", "64."] },
{ t: "Lab: file-size investigation", o: "I can investigate the sizes of real files and explain the differences.",
  k: ["file size: how much storage a file uses", "properties: details about a file, such as its size and type", "storage: where data is kept when not in use"],
  d: ["Which is bigger: a one-page text file or a photo? Why?", "The photo. It stores a colour for each of millions of pixels."],
  s: ["Finding the size|Right-click a file and choose Properties|Read the size and the unit|Record it in a table",
      "What to compare|A text file with one sentence, then with a full page|The same picture saved small and large|A short sound clip and a short video",
      "Looking for reasons|More characters, more bytes|More pixels, more bytes|Sound and video need many samples every second"],
  a: "Record the sizes of the ten sample files, then create a text file, type exactly 10 characters, save it and read its size. Add 90 more characters and compare. Write three conclusions. Support: use the results table with the file names filled in. Stretch: estimate how many of the text files would fit in the storage used by the video.",
  w: [["Where can you find the size of a file?", "In its properties."], ["A plain text file holds 50 characters. Roughly how many bytes is it?", "About 50 bytes, one per character."], ["Put in order of typical size, smallest first: video, text file, photo.", "Text file, photo, video."], ["A photo is 3 MiB. Roughly how many KiB is that?", "3 x 1024 = 3072 KiB."], ["Explain why a one-minute video is far larger than a one-minute sound clip.", "Video stores many full pictures every second as well as the sound."]],
  x: ["Write one conclusion from your investigation.", "A conclusion supported by the results."] },
{ t: "Same bits, different meaning", o: "I can explain that a bit pattern can mean different things.",
  k: ["character: a letter, digit or symbol", "ASCII: a code that gives each character a number", "pixel: one dot of a picture"],
  d: ["The digits 01000001 are stored in memory. What could they mean?", "The number 65, the letter A, or part of a colour or sound."],
  s: ["One pattern, many meanings|As a number, 01000001 is 65|As a character, 65 is the letter A in ASCII|As a colour, it could be a shade of grey",
      "Character codes|ASCII gives every character a number|A is 65, B is 66, a is 97|The computer stores the number in binary",
      "Who decides?|The program that opens the data|Open a picture in a text editor and you see nonsense|That is why files have types"],
  a: "Decode the binary message into letters using the ASCII table, then encode your first name. Colour the 8 x 8 grid using the bit patterns (1 = black, 0 = white) to reveal a picture. Support: decode the message one byte at a time with the table. Stretch: explain what happens when a text file is opened in an image program, and why.",
  w: [["What is ASCII?", "A code that gives each character a number."], ["The letter A is 65. What is its 8-bit binary code?", "01000001."], ["If A is 65, what letter is 01000011?", "C (67)."], ["Decode: 01001000 01001001.", "HI (72 and 73)."], ["Explain how the computer knows whether 01000001 is a number or a letter.", "It does not know from the bits. The program using the data decides how to interpret it."]],
  x: ["Name three kinds of data that are stored as binary.", "Any three: numbers, text, images, sound, video."] },
{ t: "Simple ciphers with binary codes", o: "I can encode and decode a message using a number code.",
  k: ["encode: to change information into a code", "decode: to change a code back into information", "cipher: a method of hiding a message"],
  d: ["If A = 1, B = 2 and so on, what word is 3, 1, 2?", "CAB."],
  s: ["Codes and ciphers|A code swaps each letter for a number|A cipher changes it again to hide the meaning|Both sides must know the rule",
      "A shift cipher|Add the same number to every letter's code|With a shift of 1, A becomes B and HELLO becomes IFMMP|To decode, subtract the shift",
      "In binary|Convert the letter's number to binary|Send the bits|The receiver converts back and reverses the shift"],
  a: "Encode a six-letter word with a shift cipher, write it in 5-bit binary, and pass it to a partner with the shift number to decode. Then try to break a message when the shift is not known. Support: use a shift of 1 and the letter chart. Stretch: explain how you could break any shift cipher by trying all the shifts.",
  w: [["What does encode mean?", "To change information into a code."], ["With a shift of 2, what does CAT become?", "ECV."], ["A message was encoded with a shift of 3. How do you decode it?", "Subtract 3 from each letter (shift back 3)."], ["If A = 1, write the letter D after a shift of 1 as a 5-bit binary number.", "E = 5 = 00101."], ["Explain why a shift cipher is easy to break.", "There are only 25 possible shifts, so someone can try them all."]],
  x: ["Decode with a shift of 1: IJ.", "HI."] },
{ t: "Lab: build a binary converter in a spreadsheet", o: "I can use formulas to build a tool that converts binary to denary.",
  k: ["spreadsheet: software that stores data in a grid and calculates with formulas", "formula: an instruction in a cell that calculates a value", "cell reference: the address of a cell, such as B2"],
  d: ["In a spreadsheet, what does the formula =A1+B1 do?", "Adds the values in cells A1 and B1."],
  s: ["Lay it out|Row 1: the place values 128, 64, 32, 16, 8, 4, 2, 1|Row 2: the user types a 0 or a 1 under each|Row 3: each cell multiplies the bit by its place value",
      "The formulas|In A3 type `=A1*A2` and fill across|Total: `=SUM(A3:H3)`|Change a bit and the total updates",
      "Make it usable|Add labels and colour the input cells|Test with 00000000, 11111111 and 01001101|Does it give 0, 255 and 77?"],
  a: "Build the binary-to-denary converter and test it with five values, recording expected and actual results. Support: use the template with the layout in place and add the formulas. Stretch: add a message that appears if a cell contains anything other than 0 or 1.",
  w: [["What is a formula?", "An instruction in a cell that calculates a value."], ["What does `=A1*A2` do?", "Multiplies the value in A1 by the value in A2."], ["Which formula adds up cells A3 to H3?", "=SUM(A3:H3)."], ["Your converter shows 78 for 01001101. The correct answer is 77. What would you check?", "The place values in row 1 and the bit typed in the last column."], ["Explain why testing with 00000000 and 11111111 is a good idea.", "They are the smallest and largest values, so they check every column and the extremes."]],
  x: ["What total does your converter give for 10101010?", "170."] },
{ t: "Revision: mixed questions", o: "I can answer mixed questions on binary and data.",
  k: ["revise: to go over learning again", "method: the steps used to solve a problem", "check: to make sure an answer is right"],
  d: ["Write the eight place values of a byte from memory.", "128, 64, 32, 16, 8, 4, 2, 1."],
  s: ["Conversions|Binary to denary: add the place values with a 1|Denary to binary: subtract the largest that fits|Always write 8 bits",
      "Addition|0+0=0, 0+1=1, 1+1=0 carry 1, 1+1+1=1 carry 1|A carry out of the left-hand column is overflow|Check in denary",
      "Units and bits|Bit, nibble, byte, KiB, MiB|n bits give 2 to the power n patterns|The largest value is one less than the number of patterns"],
  a: "Complete the mixed revision paper in pairs, then mark it with the answer sheet and list the two question types you most need to practise. Support: use the method cards. Stretch: write a five-question test with a mark scheme for another pair.",
  w: [["Convert 01100110 to denary.", "102."], ["Convert 150 to binary.", "10010110."], ["Add 00101101 + 00010110.", "01000011 (45 + 22 = 67)."], ["How many KiB are in 4096 bytes?", "4."], ["How many bits are needed to store a month of the year (12 values)? Explain.", "4 bits, because 3 bits give only 8 patterns and 4 give 16."]],
  x: ["Which two question types will you practise before the test?", "Two named types."] },
{ t: "Assessed test: conversions, addition, units", o: "I can show what I know about binary and data in a written test.",
  k: ["test conditions: working alone, in silence, without notes", "working: the steps that show how an answer was reached", "mark: a point awarded for a correct step or answer"],
  d: ["What should you always show in a conversion or addition question?", "Your working."],
  s: ["The test|30 minutes, on your own|Conversions, two additions, units and one explanation|No calculators",
      "How to gain marks|Show the place values or carries you used|Write all 8 bits|Read the question twice",
      "If you finish early|Check every conversion by converting back|Check additions in denary|Check the units question for order"],
  a: "Complete the 30-minute written test under test conditions. Support: a place-value strip is allowed. Stretch: attempt the extension question on hexadecimal at the end.",
  w: [["Convert 10110001 to denary.", "177."], ["Convert 204 to binary.", "11001100."], ["Add 01011011 + 00110110 and state whether there is overflow.", "10010001 (91 + 54 = 145). No overflow."], ["Add 11001000 + 01100100 in 8 bits and state what is stored.", "Overflow. 200 + 100 = 300. Stored: 00101100 (44)."], ["Explain why computers use binary.", "Their circuits are made of switches with two states, on and off, which are reliable and simple to tell apart."]],
  x: ["Which question were you least sure about?", "A question number."] },
{ t: "Feedback, corrections and reflection", o: "I can correct my mistakes and reflect on my learning.",
  k: ["correction: a rewritten answer that fixes a mistake", "misconception: a wrong idea that causes mistakes", "target: a specific thing to work on next"],
  d: ["Look at your first wrong answer. Was it a slip or something you did not understand?", "A decision about the type of mistake."],
  s: ["Types of mistake|Slip: you knew how, but made an error|Gap: you did not know the method|Misreading: you answered a different question",
      "Corrections|Redo each wrong question in full|Write what went wrong in a few words|Check it with the Data page of CS Toolkit",
      "Reflect|What can I do now that I could not do before?|Which method am I most confident with?|What is my target?"],
  a: "Complete corrections for every question you lost marks on, labelling each as slip, gap or misreading. Then complete the unit reflection. Support: your teacher has chosen three questions to correct. Stretch: write an explanation of binary addition for a Year 6 pupil.",
  w: [["Copy one question you got wrong and correct it in full.", "A fully corrected answer."], ["Was it a slip, a gap or a misreading?", "A labelled mistake type."], ["Name one thing you can do now that you could not do six weeks ago.", "A unit skill."], ["Which method are you most confident with?", "A named method."], ["Set one target and say how you will practise it.", "A specific target with a practice plan."]],
  x: ["In one sentence, how can a machine that only knows on and off store numbers?", "By using patterns of on and off as binary digits, where each position is worth double the one before."] }
] }); })(typeof module !== "undefined" ? require("../lessons.js") : window.Toolkit);
